Measurement remains authoritative.
Memory does NOT replace verification.
2. MEMORY ARCHITECTURE
Implement four distinct memory scopes.
2.1 Tier 1 — Foundation / Structural Memory
Purpose:
Stable knowledge about how the reasoning system itself should operate.
Examples:
reasoning constraints
stable domain rules
invariants
known structural relationships
explicitly supplied durable knowledge
Properties:
changes rarely
highest persistence
provenance required
never silently modified
mutation must be explicit and traceable
Do NOT treat Tier 1 merely as a system prompt.
Represent it as actual memory records.
2.2 Tier 2 — Learned / Domain Memory
Purpose:
Slowly consolidated reusable knowledge learned from repeated experience.
Examples:
recurring solution patterns
recurring failure modes
domain relationships
strategies repeatedly supported by measurement
Properties:
slower-changing than episodic memory
must retain provenance back to contributing episodes
must distinguish measurement-supported information from model-derived inference
promotion from Tier 3 must be conservative
Pass 4 does NOT require sophisticated autonomous consolidation.
A deterministic minimal consolidation mechanism is enough.
2.3 Tier 3 — Experiential / Episodic Memory
Purpose:
Store actual prior BAN experiences.
An episode should be able to represent:
problem/task signature
relevant input metadata
strategies attempted
branch IDs
graph path
candidate measurements
final answer measurement
outcome
failure classification
recovery events
useful branch discoveries
unsuccessful approaches
measurement authority
measurement provenance
latency/cost
timestamp
Important:
Failures are valuable memories.
Do not store only successful outcomes.
2.4 Working Memory
Working memory is the bounded subset retrieved for the current problem.
It must be:
bounded
deterministic where possible
inspectable
traceable
removable/resettable
separate from persistent storage
Working memory must never grow without bound.
Provide explicit configuration such as:
max memory records
max approximate bytes or characters
optional age window
tier inclusion
deterministic retrieval weights
